Z||000000||wxxo||08-03-2000||02:03 PM||||ok im either very stupid or maybe im brave but im going to swap trannys this weekend. i have no idea what im doing. just have a haynes manual and some info that i found on the net. im also going to print out any replies i get to this post and use them. just wondering if i have to bleed the clutch to do this. (yes i am going to use the old one) wish that i had the time and money to get a new one but thats not an option. if i did have the time and money i would just pay the 1200 bucks to have the mechanic do it haha. thanks in advance for any help.<P>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Richard Parnell||08-03-2000||02:27 PM||||If the clutch disengages ok now, all you need to do is take the slave off the bracket with hydraulic line still connected and move the slave and line out of the way.  Then reattatch to bracket on new tranny when done.||||reg||12
Z||000002||Carrolles||08-03-2000||03:21 PM||||Richard is right.  If you do it this way be sure not to push the clutch pedal in when the slave is disconnected from the trans or you will push the plunger to the end of its travel which could damage it.||||reg||1
Z||000003||webbee||08-03-2000||05:39 PM||||If you don't want/need to do an alignment disconect the axels/cv joints from the tranny, before you pull the cradle.||||reg||1
